Contract: astra-mcp-egress-v1

The production Docker application network must have exactly one IPv4 subnet
and Docker IPv6 must be disabled until an equivalent IPv6 host policy exists.
The first matching DOCKER-USER rule for that source subnet jumps to the
ASTRA_MCP_EGRESS_V1 chain.

The chain permits the application subnet for PostgreSQL, Redis, frontend and
backend traffic. It rejects non-public, loopback, link-local, carrier-grade
NAT, documentation, benchmark, multicast and reserved IPv4 destinations. It
then permits remaining public destinations on their product-selected ports;
this is required for SMTP, public HTTP retrieval, HTTPS/WSS and reviewed
public proxy integrations that share the application network. MCP itself
still requires HTTPS and validates every resolved and connected address in the
application layer. This host contract is the independent private-address and
metadata barrier, not a product-wide public-port allowlist.

The installed marker records this file's SHA-256, Docker network name and
subnet. A root-owned systemd timer reapplies the exact rule set after Docker
or host restarts. Every install, repair and verify holds one host process lock.
Repair first installs a temporary source-subnet REJECT fence;
the fence remains if any chain write or verification fails, and is removed
only after the exact body and unique first jump are verified.
Normal application deployment only verifies this contract; installing or changing
it is a separately authorized host-security operation.
